5. Address Assignment Plan

Within each subnet, address blocks are allocated based on device roles and types.

Range	Usage
.1 - .100	Gateways, Load Balancers
.101 - .200	Service Instances (JVMs)
.201 - .254	Dynamic/Ephemeral Resources

6. Naming Convention

To maintain consistency and avoid confusion, the following naming convention is adopted:

Format: env-role-location-range
Examples:
	•	dev-svc-us-east-242.0.1.0/24
	•	ai-training-cluster-248.0.10.0/24

7. IP Address Management and Documentation

All allocations will be tracked in a centralized IP address management (IPAM) tool or detailed spreadsheet, including:
	•	Subnet information
	•	Assigned IP addresses
	•	Service/Team ownership
	•	Allocation date and status

8. Future Scalability

The 245.0.0.0/8 to 247.0.0.0/8 range is reserved for future expansion. Periodic reviews will assess usage and reallocation needs.

9. Security and Access Control
	•	Network Segmentation: Ensure strict separation between environments to avoid cross-contamination.
	•	Access Control Lists (ACLs): Limit access to critical infrastructure networks based on roles.
	•	Firewall Rules: Implement environment-specific rules to manage traffic flow securely.

10. Conclusion

This allocation plan enables efficient and scalable management of the Class E IP range for private, experimental, and non-production environments. It ensures clear segmentation, supports growth, and minimizes conflict across services and teams.

